# The ledger of `compress/zstd`: what Python's `compression.zstd` (3.15)
# lets a program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. Go's
# and Rust's standard libraries have no Zstandard, so their lines are `-` in
# every block. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-18: `dir()` of
# `compression.zstd` and of its classes and enums. Every frame in the
# package's tests was made by the `zstd` tool 1.5.7 or by hand and given to
# Python first; the cross-check in the scratch folder read 21 of the tool's
# frames (levels 1, 3 and 19; text, random bytes, runs, zeros, one byte,
# none, a repeated pair) and frames of `--ultra -22 --long`, `--fast=5`,
# `-B4096`, `--no-content-size`, a pipe, two joined, bytes of a skewed
# distribution and a trained dictionary's, all alike; had the tool read
# what Rebel wrote of each, with and without a dictionary, all alike; fed
# 800 corrupted frames to the decoder, every one answered with `invalid` or
# with bytes, never an abort; and compared 600 corrupted frames with
# Python: no frame both read differently.

capability zstd/at-once  bytes compressed whole into one frame
  heroes  compress/zstd.compress
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.compress
  note    the parse is greedy over hash chains of four bytes, the sequences coded with the predefined tables, the literals Huffman-coded where they are 64 or more (the tree's weights written one by one, or compressed with FSE where a byte past 127 is among them), stored otherwise. Against `zstd -3` on the scratch folder's files it writes 54% more on its text, 33% more on RFC 7932's, 2% more on bytes of a skewed distribution, and as much on random bytes

capability zstd/decompress-at-once  frames decompressed whole under a limit
  heroes  compress/zstd.decompress
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.decompress
  note    a `limit` is required, where Python asks for none; a frame whose header says a size past it fails before a byte is decoded. Frames one after another and skippable frames between them are read as Python reads them. Bytes after the last frame and an empty input fail `invalid`, as Python's do. A Huffman stream not read exactly to its first bit fails `invalid`, as RFC 8878 section 4.2.2 says, where libzstd reads some (observed: 25 of 600 corrupted frames Python read and Rebel refused, and none read differently)

capability zstd/leading  a frame at the start of other bytes, and where it ended
  heroes  compress/zstd.decompress_leading
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.ZstdDecompressor, ZstdDecompressor.unused_data, ZstdDecompressor.eof
  note    -

capability zstd/frame-info  what a frame's header says, read without decompressing
  heroes  compress/zstd.frame_info, compress/zstd.FrameInfo, compress/zstd.ContentSize
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.get_frame_info, compression.zstd.FrameInfo, FrameInfo.decompressed_size, FrameInfo.dictionary_id
  note    an unknown size is a case of its own, where Python answers `None`; the window and the checksum flag are said too

capability zstd/frame-size  where a frame ends, its blocks walked
  heroes  compress/zstd.frame_size
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.get_frame_size
  note    -

capability zstd/skippable  a skippable frame written, and read past
  heroes  compress/zstd.skippable_frame, compress/zstd.decompress
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    libzstd's `ZSTD_writeSkippableFrame`, which Python does not offer; its reader passes such frames over

capability zstd/levels  how hard the compressor works
  heroes  compress/zstd.Options, compress/zstd.defaults, compress/zstd.MIN_LEVEL, compress/zstd.DEFAULT_LEVEL, compress/zstd.MAX_LEVEL
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.COMPRESSION_LEVEL_DEFAULT, compress(level=), ZstdCompressor(level=), CompressionParameter.compression_level
  note    1 to 22, 3 the usual as in Python; each level tries more earlier places, 2 to 2,048. Python's negative levels, faster still, are not offered; a level outside 1 to 22 fails `invalid`

capability zstd/frame-flags  the checksum and the content size written or not
  heroes  compress/zstd.Options
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  CompressionParameter.checksum_flag, CompressionParameter.content_size_flag, CompressionParameter.dict_id_flag
  note    the checksum off and the size on by default, as Python's; the dictionary's id is always written where it has one; the checksum, the low 32 bits of the frame's XXH64, is `hash.xxh64`'s since 2026-11-19 (repairs 6), fed block by block as a frame is read, where this package held a copy of its own

capability zstd/parameters  the compressor's own parameters: window, hash and chain sizes, strategy, long matching, threads
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.CompressionParameter, CompressionParameter.window_log, CompressionParameter.hash_log, CompressionParameter.chain_log, CompressionParameter.search_log, CompressionParameter.min_match, CompressionParameter.target_length, CompressionParameter.strategy, CompressionParameter.enable_long_distance_matching, CompressionParameter.ldm_hash_log, CompressionParameter.ldm_min_match, CompressionParameter.ldm_bucket_size_log, CompressionParameter.ldm_hash_rate_log, CompressionParameter.job_size, CompressionParameter.overlap_log, CompressionParameter.nb_workers, CompressionParameter.bounds, compression.zstd.Strategy, Strategy.fast, Strategy.dfast, Strategy.greedy, Strategy.lazy, Strategy.lazy2, Strategy.btlazy2, Strategy.btopt, Strategy.btultra, Strategy.btultra2
  note    libzstd's search strategies and their sizes; this encoder has one strategy, greedy, and a level. Not written in this round

capability zstd/window-limit  a bound on the window a reader accepts
  heroes  refused: the reader holds the whole output, bounded by `limit`; the window bounds only how far back a repeat reaches, which is held to the bytes before it
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.DecompressionParameter, DecompressionParameter.window_log_max, DecompressionParameter.bounds
  note    -

capability zstd/dictionaries  a dictionary read, its id and content, frames written and read with it
  heroes  compress/zstd.Dictionary, compress/zstd.dictionary, compress/zstd.raw_dictionary, compress/zstd.id_of, compress/zstd.content_of, compress/zstd.compress_with, compress/zstd.decompress_with
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.ZstdDict, ZstdDict.dict_id, ZstdDict.dict_content, ZstdDict.as_digested_dict, ZstdDict.as_undigested_dict, ZstdDict.as_prefix, ZstdDict(is_raw=), compress(zstd_dict=), decompress(zstd_dict=)
  note    a dictionary `zstd --train` wrote gives its tables and offsets to the frames read with it; the compressor reaches back into its content and names its id, its own tables staying the predefined ones. Python's digested and undigested forms are libzstd's caches of the same dictionary, and its prefix is `raw_dictionary`. A frame that names a dictionary other than the one given fails `invalid`

capability zstd/train  a dictionary trained from samples, or finished from content
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.train_dict, compression.zstd.finalize_dict
  note    libzstd's COVER trainer; not written in this round

capability zstd/compress-incremental  bytes compressed piece by piece, flushed by block or by frame
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.ZstdCompressor, ZstdCompressor.compress, ZstdCompressor.flush, ZstdCompressor.set_pledged_input_size, ZstdCompressor.last_mode, ZstdCompressor.CONTINUE, ZstdCompressor.FLUSH_BLOCK, ZstdCompressor.FLUSH_FRAME
  note    not written in this round

capability zstd/decompress-incremental  frames decompressed piece by piece, or through a stream
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  ZstdDecompressor.decompress, ZstdDecompressor.needs_input, compression.zstd.ZstdFile.read, ZstdFile.read1, ZstdFile.readinto, ZstdFile.readinto1, ZstdFile.peek
  note    a frame's blocks are at most 128 KiB, so a reader holding the window can hand out a block at a time; not written in this round

capability zstd/failures  what a broken frame answers
  heroes  compress/zstd.INVALID, compress/zstd.LIMIT
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.ZstdError
  note    `invalid` names the byte where the frame stopped being Zstandard

capability zstd/file  a Zstandard file opened by its path, read and written as a file
  heroes  refused: a path is `fs`'s, and a file's bytes are read whole by `fs.read_bytes` and written by `fs.write_bytes`
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  compression.zstd.open, compression.zstd.ZstdFile, ZstdFile.write, ZstdFile.flush, ZstdFile.close, ZstdFile.mode, ZstdFile.name, ZstdFile.closed, ZstdFile.FLUSH_BLOCK, ZstdFile.FLUSH_FRAME
  note    -

capability zstd/lines  a compressed text read line by line, or sought in
  heroes  refused: lines are `io`'s and `bytes.lines`'s over the bytes read whole; seeking is decompressing again from the start
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  ZstdFile.readline, ZstdFile.readlines, ZstdFile.seek, ZstdFile.tell, ZstdFile.seekable
  note    -

dropped
  python  ZstdFile.fileno, ZstdFile.isatty, ZstdFile.readable, ZstdFile.writable, ZstdFile.detach, ZstdFile.truncate, ZstdFile.writelines: what every Python file object has
  python  compression.zstd.zstd_version, compression.zstd.zstd_version_info: which C library Python links; Rebel stands on none
  python  compression.zstd.enum: a module Python's own `compression.zstd` imports
